I’ll inspect the pattern, metadata, and the provided tungstate guidance first, then identify candidate phases and perform a quantitative GSAS-II refinement. I’ll validate the exported CIFs, profile table, phase fractions, and project file against the final model before reporting uncertainty.

The sample is a CaCO₃ + WO₃ reaction fired at 500 °C, so the first candidates are CaWO₄ plus residual/decomposition products (WO₃, CaCO₃, and possibly CaO). The scan is raw Cu Kα data over 10–100° with 8,293 points and a substantial low-angle background; I’m now separating peak positions/intensities from that background before database matching.

Peak positions and unique reflections confirm three crystalline phases: monoclinic WO₃, scheelite CaWO₄, and calcite CaCO₃. The first quantitative fit is already near 54.8/12.8/32.4 wt% (WO₃/CaWO₄/calcite), but its unconstrained width parameters became nonphysical; I’m tightening the model to the documented instrument terms and comparing the monoclinic/triclinic WO₃ reference variants before accepting fractions.

The reference comparison strongly favors monoclinic P2₁/c WO₃ (the Loopstra–Boldrini model); triclinic WO₃ fits very poorly. Atomic-coordinate freedom lowers Rwp, but unconstrained oxygen refinement in the minor CaWO₄ phase produces an implausibly long W–O bond, so I’m rejecting that overfit and retaining chemically sound reference oxygen geometry while refining only parameters supported by the data.

